## Adversarial model panel, and two holes in the withdrawal oracle (2026-08-18)
Four read-only reviewers on the same commit, different lenses: qwen3p8-max (judge as a
hostile hackathon judge), deepseek-v4-pro (staff-engineer code review), kimi-k3 (validate
the idea and its prior art), grok-4.6-xhigh (competitive strategy). Fireworks via
opencode for the first three, cursor for grok. All four returned prose where the run
demanded JSON, so marshal recorded them failed; the analysis was intact.

The finding that mattered, reached independently by two of them:

  The withdrawal oracle was status-only (404/410) while block detection was body-aware
  from the start. So the weaker detector sat on the case this project exists to defend.

  a) A 200 response with a "no longer available" body is a withdrawal that the oracle
     read as a loss. Loss authorises a repair. The repair then set baselineRefs from
     the post-heal run, dropping the record with no withdrawn mark and no incident: a
     deliberately removed record replaced by fabricated data, silently.
  b) probeUrl maps transport failure to status 0 so a flaky network is not read as a
     withdrawal. Right instinct, but the ref then fell to lost, and lost heals. The
     safety property failed open whenever the instrument was unavailable.

Fixed: GONE_MARKERS checked on a 200 (narrow list; a false positive marks a live record
withdrawn), and a third category `unresolvedRefs` for probes that did not answer. Any
unresolved ref refuses the repair before a cause is assigned. Five new tests, including
the mixed case: two refs confirmed live, one unreachable, no repair.

Also fixed from the same panel:
  - heal_call_failed / heal_trigger_failed were recorded as healAttempted, so a repair
    that never reached the collector was logged as one that ran and was rejected. Same
    misreading as heal_busy, one door along. Now a deferral, with a test.
  - sourceCard re-derived source trust from a fresh contract run and could disagree with
    the runner, letting the feed label every record verified while its own health strip
    called the source unverified. Latent, not live. The card now applies the runner's
    reasoning: a volume breach with recorded withdrawals behind it does not un-verify
    rows that were read cleanly; a field breach still does.

Claims the panel checked and found wrong:
  - "built over six days" against a git history spanning 2026-08-17 17:50 to
    2026-08-18 16:13, about 22 hours. Corrected in docs/ai-assistance.md.
  - scraper create "128s to 306s" against create-ebay at 362s in this file. Corrected.
  - "193 real eBay listings" where the capture holds 190 distinct listing_urls across
    193 rows. Corrected to say rows, with the distinct count stated.

Claims the panel checked and found correct: 17/168/8 study figures, MTTR 347.6s and heal
330.6s against the incident file, the 21.4% cliff, the test count, the ten mutations, and
the six-tests-fail claim for the gone branch.

116 tests.

## Redirect route closed, and what the oracle still cannot see (2026-08-18)
Third way past the withdrawal oracle, from the same panel: a permalink that answers 200
after redirecting somewhere else. Marketplaces send an ended listing to a category page
or a similar product; following the redirect and reading 200 made a removed record look
present, which files it as lost, which authorises a repair. probeUrl now reports the
final URL and a changed path counts as a gone signal. Scheme upgrades, host aliases and
trailing slashes are normalised away, because treating those as withdrawals would mark
live records gone. Five tests.

Stated as unhandled rather than left for a reader to assume: in-place revision (a recall
expanded to cover more units returns 200 at the same URL with changed content and looks
healthy), semantic drift (a price that starts including shipping still parses), and any
soft-gone wording outside the deliberately narrow GONE_MARKERS list.

121 tests.

