## Property tests, and a fourth hole in the oracle (2026-08-18)
Added engine/src/classify.fuzz.test.ts: a seeded generator, 3000 inputs, five invariants.
It found a real defect on its first run, in code that four model reviews and six example
tests had already been over.

  seed 26: a permalink answering HTTP 418 was healable.

The unresolved guard was a blocklist naming 0, 5xx, 403, 429 and 408, the statuses that
had bitten us. Anything else fell through to `lost`, which authorises a repair. Now an
allowlist: a clean 200 with no gone signal is the only response that means "still
published"; everything else refuses. Grok's phrasing on the earlier pass was exactly
right, "the named holes are closed, the class of hole is not", and this is what that
looked like in practice.

The generator also flagged a case that turned out to be my property being wrong, not the
code: a withdrawn record can legitimately sit alongside a genuinely lost one, because the
repair addresses the loss while the withdrawal is already marked and preserved. Property
corrected to say what the guarantee actually is, with an added assertion that a withdrawn
ref is never also queued for repair.

Three of the four holes found in this oracle were the same mistake: enumerating what
counts as absence and letting everything else default to presence. Presence is the claim
that costs something.

132 tests, of which 5 are properties over generated input.

## Runner properties, and checking that the fuzzer checks anything (2026-08-18)
Added engine/src/runner.fuzz.test.ts: 400 seeds, five cycles each, 2000 supervision
cycles against a source that breaks, recovers, gets blocked, loses records, has them come
back, and returns nonsense from the healer. Four invariants on the persisted state:
last-good is never replaced by output that did not pass, a withdrawn ref only stops being
withdrawn by being reported as resurrected, every non-healthy cycle opens an incident and
no incident is both attempted and deferred, and no row is ever served that did not come
from this run, the heal, or last-good.

The state machine survived all four. DeepSeek and Grok had both suspected sequence bugs
there and neither found one; nor did this.

Then checked whether the classifier fuzzer was actually testing anything. It was not,
much. Distribution over 3000 inputs was blocked 1851, drift 1096, healthy 32, gone 20,
pagination 1, and only 121 inputs were healable. The headline property constrains the
healable path, and it was seeing 4% of runs. Blocked short-circuits everything before it,
so an over-weighted block probability was hollowing out the whole suite.

Rebalanced: blocked 921, drift 1822, gone 60, pagination 29, healthy 168, and 650 healable
inputs. Added a coverage test that asserts floors per cause and for the healable,
unresolved and withdrawn paths, so tuning the generator cannot quietly gut it again.

Same lesson as the output verifier, third time today: a check that reports success
without establishing the property is worse than no check, and that applies to the checks
on the checks.

137 tests, nine of them properties over generated input.

## The panel found the real one (2026-08-18)
deepseek-v4-pro, with a shell this time, found a path that republishes a withdrawn record
and proved it with a failing test rather than an argument. Reproduced on master before
touching anything.

  baseline r1..r6, extraction returns r1..r4
  r5 permalink 200 (genuine loss), r6 permalink 404 (withdrawn)
  classify -> drift, healable, withdrawnRefs [r6], lostRefs [r5]
  heal runs, returns all six rows, contract passes
  r6 served as "healed", written to lastGoodRows and back into baselineRefs

The post-repair check only re-ran the contract, and the contract measures shape. It knows
nothing about the withdrawal established seconds earlier in the same cycle. This is the
failure the project exists to prevent, reached through the repair path rather than the
classifier.

Fixed in two places. The repair result is now rejected if it contains any ref confirmed
withdrawn this cycle. And the last-good fallback is filtered by known withdrawals, because
last-good predates what we learned and serving it unfiltered republishes the same record
under an "unverified" label. Confirmed withdrawals also now leave the baseline on the
refusal paths, so the next cycle does not rediscover them.

Also from the same run: duplicate probes for one ref resolved last-wins, because
`new Map(pairs)` keeps the last entry. A ref probed [404, 200] became lost and authorised
a repair. Contradictory readings now resolve to the refusing answer. Three tests.

Its second finding, that the oracle allowlist missed 401/407/451/301/302, was already
closed by the allowlist rewrite an hour earlier; it reviewed the commit before that.

What it could not break, stated because it matters as much: every contract boundary
(zero rows, baseline zero, NaN price, absent fields, exact minRows, exact drop limit),
the pure gone and blocked refusals, the documented oracle refusals, and the rule that a
refused run never advances the baseline or last-good. The demo replay prints only
verdicts the engine actually reaches.

The part worth keeping: my own property test had explicitly permitted this, with a
comment reasoning that a withdrawn record could not come back because it was gone from
the source. The healer is the one component whose failure mode is inventing data, and I
assumed it would not.

142 tests, ten of them properties over generated input.

## Self-evolution (2026-08-18)
Field names moved out of the adapters and into learned/aliases.json, and `npm run evolve`
now derives new ones from the committed captures. The adapters ask the store; the store
is data; the store is committed, so a clone behaves identically and every change is a
readable diff.

Auto-apply rule, deliberately narrow: a name that fills a canonical field null on every
row, appended rather than inserted so it cannot change a value already being read, and
only for fields with a distinctive shape (an identifier whose values are already in the
baseline, a URL that parses, a date, a number). Everything else is a proposal carrying
its evidence. Seller identity is excluded in both directions at any confidence.

Three bugs in the first dry run, all caught before anything was written:
  - offered to read `shipping` as `currency`, because a text shape test matches any
    non-empty string
  - offered to read `sellerKey`, our own hash, as `seller`
  - read the identifier TW-33887 as a price, because the number test stripped non-digits
    before parsing, so anything containing digits parsed

All three were reversible. That is the lesson: reversibility bounds what a wrong change
costs, it does not make it right, and auto-apply needs both. Inference is now limited to
fields where a wrong match is implausible rather than merely undoable.

The test that justifies the whole thing: run against the store as it stood before
collector C existed, the evolver derives listing_id -> id and date_listed -> listedOn,
and DECLINES listing_url -> permalink. I patched that third one in by hand at the time.
It was never needed, because collector C also returns product_page_url which the store
already knew, so permalink was never null. The fill-a-null guard caught a redundant edit
a person made.

157 tests.
